Wikidata entity slice
=====================

`wikidata-slice.tsv` in this directory is derived from Wikidata.

    Source:  https://www.wikidata.org
    Export:  https://query.wikidata.org/sparql (Wikidata Query Service)
    Licence: Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication)
             https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/

Wikidata's statements, labels, aliases and sitelink counts are released under
CC0, which places them in the public domain and imposes no attribution
requirement. This notice is provenance, not a licence obligation.

The slice is a mechanical subset, produced by `scripts/build_wikidata_kb.py`:

  - only entities whose `instance of` (P31) is one of an explicit list of
    concrete organisation and software/product classes are retained (the list
    is in the script; subclass recursion is deliberately not used);
  - only entities with at least 12 Wikipedia sitelinks are retained, which is
    both a prominence floor and the size control;
  - the retained columns are the QID, a coarse type (`organization` or
    `product`), the sitelink count, the English label, and English surface
    forms drawn from `also known as` (skos:altLabel), `official name` (P1448),
    `short name` (P1813) and `nickname` (P1449);
  - descriptions are NOT retained. This is deliberate: it keeps the asset small
    and avoids pulling in Wikipedia-derived text, which is CC BY-SA rather than
    CC0;
  - surface forms are cleaned mechanically — a trailing parenthetical
    disambiguator is dropped ("... Corporation (Armonk, NY)" keeps the head),
    as are bare domain names ("ibm.com"), short digit-bearing junk ("18M") and
    forms under two characters; at most 12 aliases per entity are kept;
  - surface forms built entirely from generic organisational vocabulary are
    dropped ("International company", "team"), and an entity whose own label is
    such a description is dropped outright — these are descriptions rather than
    names, and matching on them produces confident nonsense;
  - rows are sorted by descending sitelink count then QID, for a stable,
    diffable asset.

No other transformation is applied. QIDs, labels, aliases and sitelink counts
are the Wikidata values.

Nothing in the slice is used at runtime beyond exact surface matching and the
sitelink count as a prominence prior; no network call is made at any point.

